-- title:
--   Representability of the Hilbert functor for maximal-growth h
-- statement:
--   Fix natural numbers $n,m$ and a function $h:\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N}$ subject to three hypotheses: $h(d)=\binom{n+d}{n}$ for every $d<m$; for every $e\ge m$, every field $K$ and every ideal $J$ of $K[x_0,\dots,x_n]$ that is the span of a set of forms of degree $e$, if $\dim_K\big(K[x]_e/(J\cap K[x]_e)\big)=h(e)$ then $\dim_K\big(K[x]_{e+1}/(J\cap K[x]_{e+1})\big)\le h(e+1)$; and for every $e\ge m$ some field $K$ and some such ideal $J$ generated by forms of degree $e$ attains both $\dim_K\big(K[x]_e/(J\cap K[x]_e)\big)=h(e)$ and $\dim_K\big(K[x]_{e+1}/(J\cap K[x]_{e+1})\big)=h(e+1)$. Here, for a commutative ring $A$, a point of the functor, `Point A n h`, consists of an ideal $I\subseteq A[x_0,\dots,x_n]$ stable under taking homogeneous components, such that each graded piece $A[x]_d/(I\cap A[x]_d)$ is a finite projective $A$-module whose rank at every point of $\operatorname{Spec} A$ equals $h(d)$. The conclusion has two parts. First, base change: for all commutative rings $A,B$, every ring homomorphism $\varphi:A\to B$ and every $P\in$ `Point A n h`, there is $Q\in$ `Point B n h` with $Q.I=\varphi_*(P.I)$, the ideal generated by the image of $P.I$ under $A[x]\to B[x]$. Second, representability: there exist a scheme $\mathrm{Hilb}$ and bijections $\mathrm{pt}_A:$ `Point A n h` $\;\simeq\;\mathrm{Hom}(\operatorname{Spec} A,\mathrm{Hilb})$ for all commutative rings $A$, compatible with base change in the sense that whenever $Q.I=\varphi_*(P.I)$ one has $\mathrm{pt}_B(Q)=\mathrm{pt}_A(P)\circ\operatorname{Spec}\varphi$, together with some $N$ and a closed immersion $\mathrm{Hilb}\to\operatorname{Proj}\mathbb{Z}[x_0,\dots,x_N]=\mathbb{P}^N_{\mathbb{Z}}$.
--
--   This is Gotzmann's construction of the Hilbert scheme of $\mathbb{P}^n$ for a numerical function of maximal growth, in the form: the functor of homogeneous ideals with flat graded quotient of Hilbert function $h$ is representable by a scheme admitting a closed immersion into a projective space over $\mathbb{Z}$. It is applied in [`AlgebraicGeometry.HilbertFunctor.exists_scheme_represents_and_isClosedImmersion_toProjSpace_hilbertFunctionOf`](thm.html#AlgebraicGeometry.HilbertFunctor.exists_scheme_represents_and_isClosedImmersion_toProjSpace_hilbertFunctionOf), where $h$ is taken to be the Hilbert function attached to a Hilbert polynomial with $m$ beyond the Gotzmann number, so that the growth hypotheses hold.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicGeometry_HilbertFunctor_exists_scheme_represents_and_isClosedImmersion_toProjSpace_of_maximal_growth.lean

